Sval: Windows names and checks keyed by the executable are Sval's own

Firefox and Sval run side by side without touching each other's state.
With the executable named sval.exe (MOZ_APP_NAME), App Paths,
HKCR\Applications and the executable's ShimInclusionList entry are
Sval's by name. The names and checks left over were Firefox's whatever
the executable is called:

- The ProgIDs started FirefoxHTML, FirefoxURL and FirefoxPDF. Each full
  ProgID ends in the install hash, but the installer takes any
  FirefoxHTML-* value for its own: SetHandlers leaves a file type that
  Firefox holds alone, FixBadFileAssociation deletes such a value, and
  FixShellIconHandler falls back to the unsuffixed FirefoxHTML key,
  which is an installed Firefox's. They are SvalHTML, SvalURL and
  SvalPDF in the installers and in the code that registers and checks
  them at run time (nsWindowsShellService, the default agent's
  SetDefaultBrowser, ShellService, WebProtocolHandlerRegistrar).
  SetHandlers still leaves a file type that Firefox holds to Firefox.
  ShellService takes the PDF handler over from another installation of
  Sval as it does from another installation of Firefox.
- The installer's and uninstaller's "close the app" check found any
  MozillaWindowClass or MozillaDialogClass window, which every Gecko
  application registers, so a running Firefox stopped Sval's installer.
  ManualCloseAppPrompt counts a window only when its process is this
  installation's executable.
- The uninstaller removed plugin-container.exe's ShimInclusionList entry
  and the application/x-xpinstall;app=firefox MIME type, which an
  installed Firefox holds under the same names. Sval never writes the
  MIME type, and it leaves the shim entry in place.
- The update service waited for every process named
  maintenanceservice.exe to exit after stopping, so a running Mozilla
  service delayed Sval's by up to 30 seconds. It waits for its own
  service's process by id.

